## 🏗️ Architecture

```
                    User Browser (HTTPS)
                          │
                          ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Nginx (SSL Termination)                │
│     dashboard.domain.com → :8000                        │
│     livekit.domain.com → :7880                          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                          │
          ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
          ▼               ▼               ▼
┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────┐ ┌─────────────┐
│    Dashboard    │ │   LiveKit   │ │    Redis    │
│   (systemd)     │ │   Server    │ │   (state)   │
│   Port 8000     │ │  Port 7880  │ │  Port 6379  │
└────────┬────────┘ └──────┬──────┘ └──────┬──────┘
         │                 │               │
         ▼                 ▼               │
┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────────┼──────────┐
│  Agent Worker   │ │  Docker Containers  │          │
│   (systemd)     │ │  ┌───────────────┐  │          │
│                 │ │  │  livekit-sip  │◄─┼──────────┤
└─────────────────┘ │  └───────────────┘  │          │
                    │  ┌───────────────┐  │          │
                    │  │livekit-egress │◄─┼──────────┘
                    │  └───────────────┘  │
                    └─────────────────────┘
```

### Key Design Principles

- **Fully Self-Hosted** - Runs entirely on your own infrastructure with no LiveKit Cloud dependency. All features are verified to work with self-hosted LiveKit Server — no cloud API keys, no external inference endpoints, no data sent to LiveKit Cloud
- **Hybrid Architecture** - Systemd services + Docker containers
- **Stateless Dashboard** - All data fetched from LiveKit in real-time
- **SSR with Progressive Enhancement** - Server-rendered HTML + HTMX
- **Redis Caching** - Agent config cached with 60s TTL, automatic fallback
- **Secure by Default** - Cookie-based auth, CSRF protection, security headers

---

## 📋 System Requirements

### Minimum Hardware
| Component | Requirement | Notes |
|-----------|-------------|-------|
| CPU | 2 vCPU | Audio-only recording; 4+ vCPU for video recording |
| RAM | 4 GB | Recommended 8 GB for production |
| Swap | 2 GB | Required to prevent OOM crashes during high load |
| Disk | 20 GB | More for recordings |
| OS | Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 | Tested on AWS EC2 t3.medium |

### Required Ports

#### General Access (allow all — `0.0.0.0/0`)
| Port | Protocol | Service |
|------|----------|---------|
| 80 | TCP | HTTP (redirects to HTTPS) |
| 443 | TCP | HTTPS (Dashboard + LiveKit) |
| 7881 | TCP | LiveKit TCP fallback |
| 7882 | UDP | LiveKit WebRTC |
| 50000-60000 | UDP | WebRTC media |

#### SSH (restrict to your IP only)
| Port | Protocol | Service |
|------|----------|---------|
| 22 | TCP | SSH |

> 🔒 **Lock down SSH to your own IP address only.** An open SSH port will attract brute-force login attempts. If Tronic Support needs to access your server, you will need to temporarily add their IP address as well.

#### SIP Ports — Telephony (restrict to your SIP provider IPs only)
| Port | Protocol | Service |
|------|----------|---------|
| 5060 | UDP | SIP signaling |
| 10000-20000 | UDP | RTP media |

> 🚨 **CRITICAL: Lock these ports down to your SIP provider's IP addresses only.** SIP is an inherently insecure protocol — an open SIP port will receive hundreds of hacking attempts per minute from automated scanners attempting to make fraudulent calls on your trunk. This can result in significant financial loss. Only allow traffic from your SIP trunk provider's published IP ranges (e.g., Twilio, Telnyx, Vonage). Never open these ports to `0.0.0.0/0`.

### Supported Codecs

**Audio Codecs:**
| Codec | Support | Notes |
|-------|---------|-------|
| Opus | ✅ Primary | Default for WebRTC, best quality/compression |
| G.711 μ-law (PCMU) | ✅ SIP | Standard for US telephony |
| G.711 A-law (PCMA) | ✅ SIP | Standard for international telephony |
| PCM (raw) | ✅ Internal | Used by voice pipelines/agents |

**Video Codecs:**
| Codec | Support | Notes |
|-------|---------|-------|
| VP8 | ✅ | WebRTC standard |
| VP9 | ✅ | Better compression |
| H.264 | ✅ | Hardware acceleration, wide compatibility |
| AV1 | ✅ | Newest, best compression |

**Audio File Formats (for hold music/play audio):**
- MP3, WAV, OGG (max 10MB)

> **Note:** LiveKit automatically transcodes between Opus (WebRTC) and G.711 (SIP) for telephony calls. No manual codec configuration is required.

---

## 🚀 Quick Start Installation

### Prerequisites
- Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04 server
- Python 3.11 - 3.13 (3.14+ not yet supported)
- Domain names pointed to server (e.g., `dashboard.yourdomain.com`, `livekit.yourdomain.com`)
- Minimum 4GB RAM (8GB recommended for production)

### Step 1: System Setup

```bash
# Update system
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y curl git build-essential python3-pip python3-dev python3-venv

# Create swap file (required to prevent OOM crashes)
sudo fallocate -l 2G /swapfile
sudo chmod 600 /swapfile
sudo mkswap /swapfile
sudo swapon /swapfile
echo '/swapfile none swap sw 0 0' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab

# Set swappiness to 0 (only use swap as emergency overflow, not proactively)
sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=0
echo 'vm.swappiness=0' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-swappiness.conf
```

> **Note:** The `scripts/setup.sh` convenience script does NOT create a swap file automatically. You must create the swap file manually using the commands above. A swap file is required to prevent out-of-memory (OOM) crashes during high load periods. The swappiness value of 0 ensures swap is only used as emergency overflow when physical RAM is exhausted, rather than proactively moving data to disk.

```bash
# Install Poetry
curl -sSL https://install.python-poetry.org | python3 -
export PATH="/home/ubuntu/.local/bin:$PATH"
echo 'export PATH="/home/ubuntu/.local/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.bashrc
```

### Step 2: Install Redis

```bash
sudo apt install -y redis-server

# Generate password
REDIS_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
echo "Redis Password: $REDIS_PASSWORD" >> ~/livekit-credentials.txt

# Configure authentication
sudo sed -i "s/# requirepass foobared/requirepass $REDIS_PASSWORD/" /etc/redis/redis.conf
sudo systemctl restart redis-server
sudo systemctl enable redis-server
```

### Step 3: Install LiveKit Server

```bash
curl -sSL https://get.livekit.io | bash

# Generate credentials
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
echo "LiveKit API Key: $LIVEKIT_API_KEY" >> ~/livekit-credentials.txt
echo "LiveKit API Secret: $LIVEKIT_API_SECRET" >> ~/livekit-credentials.txt

# Create config
sudo mkdir -p /etc/livekit
sudo tee /etc/livekit/config.yaml > /dev/null << EOF
port: 7880
bind_addresses:
  - "127.0.0.1"

rtc:
  port_range_start: 50000
  port_range_end: 60000
  use_external_ip: true
  udp_port: 7882

redis:
  address: localhost:6379
  password: $REDIS_PASSWORD
  db: 0

keys:
  $LIVEKIT_API_KEY: $LIVEKIT_API_SECRET

room:
  auto_create: true
  empty_timeout: 60
EOF
```

### Step 4: Install Dashboard

```bash
cd /home/ubuntu
git clone https://github.com/ritzy1978/livekit-dashboard.git
cd livekit-dashboard

poetry install

# Install sentiment analysis package (for Caller Sentiment Journey feature)
poetry run pip install vaderSentiment

# Generate credentials
ADMIN_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
APP_SECRET_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
echo "Admin Password: $ADMIN_PASSWORD" >> ~/livekit-credentials.txt

# Create .env file
cat > .env << EOF
LIVEKIT_URL=https://livekit.yourdomain.com
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=$LIVEKIT_API_KEY
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=$LIVEKIT_API_SECRET
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=$ADMIN_PASSWORD
APP_SECRET_KEY=$APP_SECRET_KEY
REDIS_URL=redis://:$REDIS_PASSWORD@localhost:6379/0
DEBUG=false
HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8000
ENABLE_SIP=true
EOF
```

### Step 5: Create Systemd Services

```bash
# LiveKit Server service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=LiveKit Server
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/livekit-server --config /etc/livekit/config.yaml
Restart=always
LimitNOFILE=65536

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Dashboard service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit-dashboard.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=LiveKit Dashboard
After=network.target livekit.service redis-server.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard
Environment="PATH=/home/ubuntu/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin"
ExecStart=/home/ubuntu/.local/bin/poetry run uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Agent Worker service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit-agent.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=LiveKit Agent Worker
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard
EnvironmentFile=/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/.env
ExecStart=/home/ubuntu/.local/bin/poetry run python scripts/run_agents.py start --url ws://127.0.0.1:7880
Restart=always
StandardOutput=append:/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs/agent-service.log
StandardError=append:/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs/agent-service-error.log
CPUQuota=80%
MemoryMax=768M

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Create logs directory
mkdir -p /home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs

# Enable services
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable livekit livekit-dashboard livekit-agent

# Disable swap for LiveKit services (keeps them in RAM for performance)
sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/livekit.service.d
sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/livekit-agent.service.d
sudo mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/livekit-dashboard.service.d
echo -e "[Service]\nMemorySwapMax=0" | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit.service.d/no-swap.conf
echo -e "[Service]\nMemorySwapMax=0" | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit-agent.service.d/no-swap.conf
echo -e "[Service]\nMemorySwapMax=0" | sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/livekit-dashboard.service.d/no-swap.conf
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Start services
sudo systemctl start livekit livekit-dashboard livekit-agent
```

### Step 6: Configure Log Rotation

```bash
sudo tee /etc/logrotate.d/livekit-dashboard > /dev/null << 'EOF'
# Uses copytruncate because the agent service holds open file descriptors
# via systemd StandardOutput=append:. A rename would leave the service
# writing to the old (renamed) file while the new file stays empty.
/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs/agent-service.log
/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs/agent-service-error.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    maxsize 10M
    compress
    delaycompress
    notifempty
    missingok
    copytruncate
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y%m%d
}

/home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard/logs/agent_*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    maxsize 5M
    compress
    delaycompress
    notifempty
    missingok
    create 0644 ubuntu ubuntu
    dateext
    dateformat -%Y%m%d
}
EOF
```

### Step 7: Install Nginx and SSL

```bash
sudo apt install -y nginx certbot python3-certbot-nginx

# Create Nginx configs (replace yourdomain.com)
sudo certbot --nginx -d dashboard.yourdomain.com -d livekit.yourdomain.com \
  --non-interactive --agree-tos -m your-email@example.com --redirect
```

### Step 8: Install Docker Services (SIP & Egress)

```bash
sudo apt install -y docker.io
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

# SIP Service
sudo mkdir -p /etc/livekit-sip
sudo tee /etc/livekit-sip/config.yaml > /dev/null << EOF
log_level: info
api_key: $LIVEKIT_API_KEY
api_secret: $LIVEKIT_API_SECRET
ws_url: ws://127.0.0.1:7880
redis:
  address: 127.0.0.1:6379
  password: $REDIS_PASSWORD
  db: 0
sip:
  port: 5060
EOF

sudo docker run -d --name livekit-sip --restart unless-stopped --network host \
  -v /etc/livekit-sip/config.yaml:/sip/config.yaml:ro livekit/sip:latest

# Egress Service (requires 4 vCPU for video recording)
sudo mkdir -p /etc/livekit-egress /var/livekit/egress
sudo tee /etc/livekit-egress/config.yaml > /dev/null << EOF
log_level: info
api_key: $LIVEKIT_API_KEY
api_secret: $LIVEKIT_API_SECRET
ws_url: ws://127.0.0.1:7880
redis:
  address: 127.0.0.1:6379
  password: $REDIS_PASSWORD
  db: 0
health_port: 8080
file_output:
  local: true
  output_directory: /out
EOF

sudo docker run -d --name livekit-egress --restart unless-stopped --network host \
  -v /etc/livekit-egress/config.yaml:/config.yaml:ro \
  -v /var/livekit/egress:/out livekit/egress:latest
```

### Step 9: Initialize Security (Fail2ban & Sudoers)

```bash
# Install fail2ban
sudo apt install -y fail2ban

# Run the security setup script
cd /home/ubuntu/livekit-dashboard
python3 -m app.scripts.init_security

# Restart dashboard to enable security logging
sudo systemctl restart livekit-dashboard

# Verify fail2ban is running with LiveKit jails
sudo fail2ban-client status
```

**What init_security.py configures:**
- Fail2ban filters and jails for intrusion detection
- Passwordless sudo for dashboard commands:
  - Service management (restart livekit-dashboard, livekit-agent, livekit, fail2ban)
  - Time/NTP (set timezone, chrony sync)
  - System config (swappiness, fail2ban configs)

This enables the dashboard to manage services without requiring SSH access.

> **IMPORTANT:** After setup, go to **Settings > Security** and add your IP address to the whitelist to prevent accidental lockout.

### Step 10: Verify Installation

```bash
# Check all services
sudo systemctl status redis-server livekit livekit-dashboard livekit-agent fail2ban
sudo docker ps

# Check fail2ban jails
sudo fail2ban-client status

# View credentials
cat ~/livekit-credentials.txt
```

Access your dashboard at `https://dashboard.yourdomain.com`

